WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.300
Why would a body kill its own cells?

2
00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:09.780
Death is a normal, genetically controlled part of building a body.

3
00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:14.180
Apoptosis uses caspases, proteins that cut other proteins.

4
00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:17.320
Cell loss balances the cells that are made.

5
00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:22.160
In the hermaphrodite worm, 131 cells die on schedule.

6
00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:24.900
The same cells die in every animal.

7
00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:29.920
In healthy tissue, dead cells are cleared so fast that you rarely see a corpse.

8
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:34.240
But a repeated pattern does not prove a genetic program.

9
00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:36.680
The test is to change the genes.

10
00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:41.500
Without ced-3 or ced-4, cells normally fated to die survive.

11
00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:43.280
Some even differentiate.

12
00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:45.420
The death itself has failed.

13
00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:48.040
CED-3 turned out to be a caspase.

14
00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:49.960
CED-9 protects cells.

15
00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:53.760
Worm genetics had found the core of human death machinery.

16
00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:59.660
In apoptosis, cells shrink, their nuclei fragment, and their membranes stay intact

17
00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:00.660
until late.

18
00:01:01.283 --> 00:01:03.403
Necrotic looks do not mean an accident.

19
00:01:04.383 --> 00:01:07.423
Swelling and rupture can also follow dedicated machinery.

20
00:01:08.443 --> 00:01:11.543
Necroptosis needs RIPK3 and MLKL.

21
00:01:11.543 --> 00:01:14.323
Pyroptosis uses gasdermin pores.

22
00:01:15.363 --> 00:01:18.903
Ferroptosis depends on iron and severe lipid peroxidation.

23
00:01:19.923 --> 00:01:21.843
These are distinct regulated deaths.

24
00:01:23.043 --> 00:01:24.043
Appearance names an endpoint.

25
00:01:24.823 --> 00:01:28.763
To identify the cause, perturb the machinery and see whether the death changes.

26
00:01:29.867 --> 00:01:31.187
Now follow the cleanup.

27
00:01:32.007 --> 00:01:34.827
A dying cell calls a phagocyte with find-me signals.

28
00:01:35.887 --> 00:01:38.467
Caspases open channels that release nucleotides.

29
00:01:39.227 --> 00:01:44.487
The cell also exposes phosphatidylserine, a lipid normally kept inside its membrane.

30
00:01:45.327 --> 00:01:46.547
That is an eat-me signal.

31
00:01:47.367 --> 00:01:51.907
A phagocyte recognizes the cell, engulfs it, and digests it in lysosomes.

32
00:01:52.787 --> 00:01:57.347
In culture, without a phagocyte, apoptotic cells generally become leaky.

33
00:01:57.347 --> 00:01:59.327
That is secondary necrosis.

34
00:02:00.367 --> 00:02:01.927
How do we measure these events?

35
00:02:02.927 --> 00:02:06.847
Annexin V binds phosphatidylserine that is reachable from outside.

36
00:02:07.827 --> 00:02:09.827
Propidium iodide is a DNA dye.

37
00:02:10.707 --> 00:02:13.047
Its entry means the membrane is leaky.

38
00:02:13.647 --> 00:02:15.527
Neither marker tells you what killed the cell.

39
00:02:16.687 --> 00:02:20.467
Cells positive for both could be late apoptotic or necrotic from the start.

40
00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:23.870
So combine markers and follow them in time.

41
00:02:24.770 --> 00:02:27.450
Apoptotic cells expose the lipid before they leak.

42
00:02:28.190 --> 00:02:30.170
But necroptotic cells can do that too.

43
00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:34.150
A time course helps; it cannot settle the mechanism alone.

44
00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:36.690
One marker reports one event.

45
00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:42.250
Genetic control, distinct machinery, and clearance explain the biology behind the

46
00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:42.530
signal.
